1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,554
A lot of students who run out
of time on the SAT aren't slow.

2
00:00:03,574 --> 00:00:07,269
They spend four minutes on one
question early in the module,

3
00:00:07,289 --> 00:00:09,611
and then they rush the last five.

4
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:13,569
In this video, you'll get the
exact time budget for each section,

5
00:00:13,589 --> 00:00:18,140
a two-pass method for every module,
when to mark a question and move on,

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,945
how Desmos fits in, and what
to do in the last two minutes.

7
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:25,018
At the end, there's a short pacing
drill you can do with free official

8
00:00:25,038 --> 00:00:28,324
questions, so you can practice
all of this before test day.

9
00:00:28,347 --> 00:00:29,541
Start with the numbers.

10
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:33,577
The Reading and Writing section is
fifty-four questions in sixty-four minutes,

11
00:00:33,597 --> 00:00:35,005
split into two modules.

12
00:00:35,025 --> 00:00:38,917
So each module is twenty-seven
questions in thirty-two minutes.

13
00:00:38,975 --> 00:00:42,919
Thirty-two minutes divided by twenty-seven
questions is about seventy-one

14
00:00:42,939 --> 00:00:44,419
seconds per question.

15
00:00:44,439 --> 00:00:45,846
A little over a minute.

16
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:48,917
Math is forty-four questions
in seventy minutes.

17
00:00:48,937 --> 00:00:51,953
Each module is twenty-two
questions in thirty-five minutes.

18
00:00:51,973 --> 00:00:54,346
That's about ninety-five
seconds per question,

19
00:00:54,366 --> 00:00:56,259
a little over a minute and a half.

20
00:00:56,349 --> 00:00:59,436
But those are averages, not a
speed limit for every question.

21
00:00:59,456 --> 00:01:01,400
Some questions take twenty seconds.

22
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:03,079
Some take three minutes.

23
00:01:03,099 --> 00:01:06,063
Your job is to keep the
whole module on budget,

24
00:01:06,099 --> 00:01:07,884
not every single question.

25
00:01:07,943 --> 00:01:11,602
Next, it helps to know how College
Board orders the questions,

26
00:01:11,622 --> 00:01:14,372
because it changes how
you pace each section.

27
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:19,040
In math, College Board says the questions
in each module are arranged from

28
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:22,988
easiest to hardest, with all
four math domains mixed in.

29
00:01:23,203 --> 00:01:25,504
So the early questions should go faster,

30
00:01:25,524 --> 00:01:28,881
and the time you save there pays
for the hard ones at the end.

31
00:01:28,911 --> 00:01:30,713
Reading and Writing works differently.

32
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:34,641
College Board says questions that test
similar skills are grouped together and

33
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:37,196
arranged from easiest to hardest.

34
00:01:37,232 --> 00:01:39,069
So the module comes in runs.

35
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:42,391
A run of one question type
climbs from easier to harder,

36
00:01:42,411 --> 00:01:44,376
and then a new type starts.

37
00:01:44,415 --> 00:01:48,415
That means difficulty resets every
time the question type changes.

38
00:01:49,201 --> 00:01:53,522
A brutal vocabulary question doesn't
mean the rest of the module gets harder.

39
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:55,918
The next group starts easier again.

40
00:01:55,938 --> 00:01:59,989
So don't let one hard question steal the
time you need for the easier questions

41
00:02:00,009 --> 00:02:01,698
waiting in the next groups.

42
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:02,718
One more thing.

43
00:02:02,754 --> 00:02:06,040
The second module adapts to
how you did on the first.

44
00:02:06,147 --> 00:02:09,162
If module two feels harder, don't panic,

45
00:02:09,182 --> 00:02:10,626
and don't change your plan.

46
00:02:10,646 --> 00:02:12,647
The pacing method stays the same.

47
00:02:12,689 --> 00:02:13,832
Here's the method.

48
00:02:13,868 --> 00:02:16,367
Every module gets two passes.

49
00:02:16,393 --> 00:02:18,694
Pass one: go through
every question in order.

50
00:02:18,714 --> 00:02:21,159
If you know how to do
it, do it, answer it,

51
00:02:21,179 --> 00:02:22,087
and move on.

52
00:02:22,107 --> 00:02:24,765
If it stalls you, pick
your best answer anyway,

53
00:02:24,785 --> 00:02:27,099
mark it for review, and keep going.

54
00:02:27,119 --> 00:02:32,404
Bluebook has a mark for review tool,
a bookmark icon on each question.

55
00:02:32,547 --> 00:02:36,742
And the question menu shows which
questions you skipped or marked,

56
00:02:36,762 --> 00:02:38,892
and lets you jump straight to any of them.

57
00:02:38,912 --> 00:02:40,876
Notice the rule in pass one.

58
00:02:41,018 --> 00:02:44,356
Never leave a question blank,
even one you're marking.

59
00:02:44,376 --> 00:02:45,964
Pick something first.

60
00:02:45,984 --> 00:02:48,499
That way, if time runs
out before you get back,

61
00:02:48,519 --> 00:02:50,243
you still have an answer down.

62
00:02:50,263 --> 00:02:54,585
Pass two: open the question menu and
go back to your marked questions.

63
00:02:54,656 --> 00:02:58,350
Now you're spending your leftover
minutes on questions you already started,

64
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:00,635
with a guess already locked in.

65
00:03:00,655 --> 00:03:03,656
That beats rushing questions
you never even read.

66
00:03:03,676 --> 00:03:06,583
Aim to finish pass one with
about five minutes left.

67
00:03:06,603 --> 00:03:09,191
You can hide the timer
if it stresses you out,

68
00:03:09,211 --> 00:03:12,513
but when five minutes remain,
Bluebook gives you an alert.

69
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:15,461
Treat that alert as your
signal to start pass two.

70
00:03:15,494 --> 00:03:18,260
So how do you know a question has stalled?

71
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,030
Use a time limit that's a
bit longer than the average.

72
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:24,764
For Reading and Writing, if you've spent
about ninety seconds and still can't

73
00:03:24,784 --> 00:03:28,406
get it down to one answer,
choose the best of what's left,

74
00:03:28,426 --> 00:03:30,199
mark it, and move on.

75
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:32,556
For math, use about two minutes.

76
00:03:32,576 --> 00:03:36,791
If you're two minutes in and you
don't have a clear path to the answer,

77
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:39,255
guess, mark, and move.

78
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:40,975
Time isn't the only signal.

79
00:03:40,995 --> 00:03:45,261
Mark and move when you've read a passage
twice and still can't say what it's

80
00:03:45,281 --> 00:03:45,940
about.

81
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:48,940
When you keep going back and
forth between two choices.

82
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,674
Or when your algebra has turned into
a mess and you'd have to start over.

83
00:03:52,744 --> 00:03:56,009
This is what stops most
people: it feels like quitting.

84
00:03:56,029 --> 00:03:57,029
It isn't.

85
00:03:57,244 --> 00:03:58,724
You're not giving up on the question.

86
00:03:58,744 --> 00:04:00,867
You're moving it to the back of the line,

87
00:04:00,887 --> 00:04:04,494
where it can't block the
easier questions behind it.

88
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:05,368
Two exceptions.

89
00:04:05,388 --> 00:04:07,690
In math, the last few
questions are the hardest,

90
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,475
so you'll often spend more
than two minutes on them.

91
00:04:10,495 --> 00:04:12,440
That's fine if you banked time early.

92
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,760
And in Reading and Writing, don't mark
a question just because it's long.

93
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:17,387
Long isn't the same as hard.

94
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:22,742
The built-in Desmos calculator is
available for the whole math section.

95
00:04:22,762 --> 00:04:25,598
Used well, it saves you time.

96
00:04:25,618 --> 00:04:27,747
Used for everything, it wastes it.

97
00:04:27,767 --> 00:04:30,926
Desmos is fast when the algebra is slow.

98
00:04:30,946 --> 00:04:34,910
For a system of equations, graph
both and click the intersection.

99
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:37,568
For how many solutions,
count the crossings.

100
00:04:37,588 --> 00:04:41,231
For a vertex or x
intercepts, click the points.

101
00:04:41,272 --> 00:04:44,180
Desmos is slow when the math is quick.

102
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:47,145
If you can do it in your head
in about fifteen seconds,

103
00:04:47,165 --> 00:04:52,081
like two x equals fourteen,
typing it in just costs you time.

104
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:54,296
Desmos is also great in pass two.

105
00:04:54,316 --> 00:04:57,724
If you solved a question
by hand and it felt shaky,

106
00:04:57,744 --> 00:05:00,046
graphing it is a fast way to check.

107
00:05:00,066 --> 00:05:04,029
For the full set of shortcuts, see
the Desmos video on this channel.

108
00:05:04,062 --> 00:05:05,506
One setup tip.

109
00:05:05,526 --> 00:05:08,542
You can drag the calculator
anywhere on the screen.

110
00:05:08,562 --> 00:05:13,454
Park it next to the question so you aren't
opening and closing it over and over.

111
00:05:13,513 --> 00:05:16,942
Checking the clock after every
question wastes time too.

112
00:05:17,085 --> 00:05:19,978
Instead, use one checkpoint per module.

113
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:22,536
In Reading and Writing,
check at the halfway point.

114
00:05:22,556 --> 00:05:26,577
With sixteen minutes left, you want
to be around question fourteen.

115
00:05:26,597 --> 00:05:29,256
In math, aim to be ahead
of the halfway pace,

116
00:05:29,276 --> 00:05:31,327
since the hard questions come at the end.

117
00:05:31,347 --> 00:05:34,834
Try to be done with question eleven when
there are about twenty minutes left.

118
00:05:34,854 --> 00:05:35,549
Let's try one.

119
00:05:35,569 --> 00:05:36,905
You're in a math module.

120
00:05:36,925 --> 00:05:40,727
The clock says ten minutes left, and
you're just starting question fifteen of

121
00:05:40,747 --> 00:05:41,747
twenty-two.

122
00:05:41,996 --> 00:05:43,693
Are you ahead or behind?

123
00:05:43,713 --> 00:05:45,213
Pause and try it.

124
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:48,841
Behind.

125
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:52,198
You have eight questions left, and
they're the hardest ones in the module,

126
00:05:52,218 --> 00:05:53,770
with only ten minutes.

127
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:55,877
That's about seventy-five seconds each,

128
00:05:55,897 --> 00:05:58,754
well under the ninety-five second average.

129
00:05:58,815 --> 00:05:59,795
So tighten up.

130
00:05:59,815 --> 00:06:02,438
Cut your mark and move
limit to about a minute,

131
00:06:02,458 --> 00:06:03,545
and keep moving.

132
00:06:03,565 --> 00:06:06,390
Every question needs an
answer before time runs out.

133
00:06:06,410 --> 00:06:07,854
Now Reading and Writing.

134
00:06:07,874 --> 00:06:12,374
Sixteen minutes left, and you're starting
question eighteen of twenty-seven.

135
00:06:12,446 --> 00:06:14,053
Ahead or behind?

136
00:06:14,089 --> 00:06:15,589
Pause and try it.

137
00:06:18,809 --> 00:06:19,809
Ahead.

138
00:06:19,916 --> 00:06:22,896
You've done seventeen
questions in sixteen minutes,

139
00:06:22,916 --> 00:06:26,717
and only ten are left, with
sixteen minutes to do them.

140
00:06:26,737 --> 00:06:27,896
Don't speed up.

141
00:06:27,916 --> 00:06:30,178
Use that time to read carefully.

142
00:06:30,198 --> 00:06:32,357
Now the last two minutes of any module.

143
00:06:32,377 --> 00:06:35,305
This is where a lot of
students leave points behind.

144
00:06:35,325 --> 00:06:38,377
First, there's no penalty
for guessing on the SAT.

145
00:06:38,397 --> 00:06:42,483
College Board says it directly: it's
better to answer every question.

146
00:06:42,503 --> 00:06:44,555
A blank is always wrong.

147
00:06:44,575 --> 00:06:45,990
A guess might be right.

148
00:06:46,010 --> 00:06:49,918
So with about two minutes left,
stop working on any long problem.

149
00:06:49,938 --> 00:06:53,776
Open the question menu and
look for anything unanswered.

150
00:06:53,796 --> 00:06:55,908
Give every blank an answer.

151
00:06:55,928 --> 00:06:59,372
For multiple choice, if you
can eliminate even one choice,

152
00:06:59,392 --> 00:07:00,730
pick from what's left.

153
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:02,821
If you can't, just pick one.

154
00:07:02,893 --> 00:07:05,050
For math questions where
you type the answer,

155
00:07:05,070 --> 00:07:06,000
enter your best estimate.

156
00:07:07,107 --> 00:07:09,173
A blank box can't earn credit.

157
00:07:09,193 --> 00:07:12,280
If you still have time after that, go
back to the marked question you were

158
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:12,851
closest on.

159
00:07:12,871 --> 00:07:14,101
Not the hardest one.

160
00:07:14,121 --> 00:07:15,958
The one where you were
down to two choices,

161
00:07:15,978 --> 00:07:17,789
or almost done with the algebra.

162
00:07:17,809 --> 00:07:20,647
And don't change answers
just because you're nervous.

163
00:07:20,667 --> 00:07:23,702
Change one only if you
found a specific mistake.

164
00:07:23,744 --> 00:07:27,724
Remember, you can move back and forth
between questions in a module until time

165
00:07:27,744 --> 00:07:28,744
expires.

166
00:07:28,851 --> 00:07:31,279
But once you leave a module,
you can't go back to it.

167
00:07:31,315 --> 00:07:33,759
So treat the end of every
module as your last chance,

168
00:07:33,779 --> 00:07:34,843
because it is.

169
00:07:34,863 --> 00:07:36,914
Here's a drill to practice all of this.

170
00:07:36,934 --> 00:07:41,328
You need the free College Board
Question Bank and a timer.

171
00:07:41,383 --> 00:07:44,935
For Reading and Writing, pick thirteen
questions across different skills,

172
00:07:44,955 --> 00:07:46,542
at mixed difficulty.

173
00:07:46,562 --> 00:07:49,363
Export them without answers
so there are no spoilers.

174
00:07:49,383 --> 00:07:51,113
Set a timer for sixteen minutes.

175
00:07:51,133 --> 00:07:52,955
That's about half a module.

176
00:07:53,005 --> 00:07:56,092
For math, pick eleven
questions: a few easy,

177
00:07:56,112 --> 00:07:59,148
some medium, and a few
hard, in that order.

178
00:07:59,184 --> 00:08:01,842
Set a timer for seventeen
and a half minutes.

179
00:08:01,862 --> 00:08:04,111
That's exactly half a module.

180
00:08:04,164 --> 00:08:05,751
Use the two-pass method.

181
00:08:05,771 --> 00:08:09,573
Guess on anything that stalls, put
a star next to it on your paper,

182
00:08:09,593 --> 00:08:10,644
and keep going.

183
00:08:10,664 --> 00:08:14,343
When you finish pass one, write
down how much time is left.

184
00:08:14,383 --> 00:08:18,148
When the timer ends, export again
with answers and explanations,

185
00:08:18,168 --> 00:08:19,168
and check.

186
00:08:19,347 --> 00:08:21,077
Write down three numbers.

187
00:08:21,097 --> 00:08:22,363
Did you answer everything?

188
00:08:22,383 --> 00:08:25,219
How much time was left after pass one?

189
00:08:25,239 --> 00:08:28,055
And how many starred
questions did you get right?

190
00:08:28,075 --> 00:08:32,197
If you didn't finish, your mark
and move limit is too loose.

191
00:08:32,217 --> 00:08:35,019
If you finished with lots of
time but missed easy questions,

192
00:08:35,039 --> 00:08:36,982
slow down on pass one.

193
00:08:37,002 --> 00:08:41,193
Do this two or three times a week,
and pacing starts to feel normal.

194
00:08:41,213 --> 00:08:42,122
Quick recap.

195
00:08:42,142 --> 00:08:45,157
About seventy-one seconds per
Reading and Writing question,

196
00:08:45,177 --> 00:08:47,514
and about ninety-five per math question.

197
00:08:47,534 --> 00:08:48,586
Two passes.

198
00:08:48,606 --> 00:08:51,729
Mark and move at about ninety
seconds or two minutes.

199
00:08:51,749 --> 00:08:54,049
Desmos when the algebra is slow.

200
00:08:54,069 --> 00:08:57,070
And with two minutes left,
answer every question.

201
00:08:57,119 --> 00:08:58,869
If this helped, subscribe.

202
00:08:59,261 --> 00:09:02,512
The next video turns
all of this into a plan:

203
00:09:02,619 --> 00:09:06,277
SAT study plans for two weeks, one month,

204
00:09:06,297 --> 00:09:10,119
and three months, using only
free official resources.
